# StrictDoc Requirement IDs in Commit Messages

## Requirement

Once a project contains StrictDoc requirements (any `.sdoc` file), every git commit message SHALL name the requirements that commit completes.

This section is in addition to the technical attribution in `git/commit-format.mdc`. Prompt, context, justification, model, IDE, generation method, code style, dependencies, and token estimate SHALL remain.

Requirement IDs in the code SHALL remain, in a comment such as `/* Implements: SSS012 */` or `/* Verifies: SSS012 */`. The comment and the commit section are both required. See `requirements-strictdoc/practices.mdc`.

Before any StrictDoc requirement exists, omit the `Requirements:` section.

## Message Section

Place the section after the change list and before the `-----` separator:

```
Requirements:
- Implements SSS012: The software shall open the contactor within 100 ms when DC bus voltage exceeds the trip threshold.
```

Each requirement line SHALL be `- <role> <UID>: <one-line statement>`.

Roles:

- `Specifies` — this commit adds or changes that requirement in an `.sdoc` file
- `Implements` — this commit's code is what satisfies that requirement
- `Verifies` — this commit's test is what checks that requirement

The UID SHALL be copied from the requirement (`UID`, or the project's requirement ID such as `SSS012`). SHALL NOT invent a UID.

The statement SHALL be that requirement's `STATEMENT`, shortened to one line without changing its meaning. A reader of `git log` SHALL see which requirement was completed without opening the diff or the `.sdoc` file.

A commit that specifies, implements, and verifies none of the requirements SHALL use exactly:

```
Requirements:
- None
```

List every requirement the commit actually completes, one per line. SHALL NOT list a requirement the commit does not specify, implement, or verify.

When one atomic commit both updates a requirement and lands its code (Exception 2 in `git/commit-atomicity.mdc`), list each role on its own line.

## Why This Works

Commits are already one change each (`git/commit-atomicity.mdc`). Naming the requirements in the message makes that commit the record of the requirements it completed. `git log --grep=SSS012` finds those commits.

## Example

```
Open the contactor on DC overvoltage

- Trip the contactor when bus voltage exceeds the threshold
- Record the SSS012 link on the trip function

Requirements:
- Implements SSS012: The software shall open the contactor within 100 ms when DC bus voltage exceeds the trip threshold.

-----
Prompt: {{prompt}}
Context: {{brief_description_of_what_this_code_does}}
Justification: {{justification_of_changes}}

Technical details:
- Model: {{specific_llm_model}}
- IDE: Cursor {{cursor_version}}
- Generation method: AI-assisted pair programming
- Code style: {{language_specific_style_guide}}
- Dependencies: {{key_dependencies}}
- Tokens: {{estimated_tokens_used_for_changes}}
```

